**Test filters.** For this step these gates run only the tests their filter selects (plus any inherited expected-red tests); put the tests this step adds or changes where a filter value finds them:
- `constructor-unit-tests`: 

**How to implement.** One window per kit, guarded by `.oc/lock` (001 §E 'a .oc/lock (machine, user, process, time) warns across processes and machines, with an open read-only choice'; 001 §A '.oc/ app-owned cache and lock; safe to delete'; the S4.1 brief: a foreign live lock is KitLockedErr carrying the holder, and the caller can then open read-only). (1) In `Kit.fs`, declared above `KitError` (step 122), each doc one to three lines: `MachineName of string` and `UserName of string`; `KitLockOwner = { machine : MachineName; user : UserName; processId : ProcessId }`, who opened a kit, where `ProcessId` is `Softellect.Sys.Primitives.ProcessId` (STL `Sys/Primitives.fs:802`), reused and not declared again (ELEV014); and `KitLockHolder = { owner : KitLockOwner; acquiredAt : ClockTime }`, with `member this.describe : string` = `<user> on <machine> (process <id>) since <yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss> UTC`, the time in the invariant culture. `KitError` gains `KitLockedErr of holder : KitLockHolder`, rendered `the kit is open elsewhere: <holder's describe>`. (2) `KitProxy` gains two fields, each with a one-line doc. `acquireLock : ClockTime -> Result<KitLockHolder, KitError>` takes the kit's lock for this proxy at that time and answers the holder. `releaseLock : unit -> Result<unit, KitError>` gives it back. Both implementations keep one rule. A lock is this proxy's when this proxy took it; any other lock is foreign, even one taken in the same process, because two windows on one kit are two holders. Acquiring while this proxy holds the lock answers its holder again and writes nothing. Acquiring when there is no lock, or a stale one, writes `{ owner = <this proxy's owner>; acquiredAt = time }` into `.oc/lock`, creating `.oc` when missing (`.oc` is created on the first write that needs it and never before), and answers it. Acquiring against a foreign live lock answers `KitLockedErr` of its holder and writes nothing; the caller can then open the kit read-only (step 131). Releasing deletes `.oc/lock` when this proxy holds it and the file still names its holder, and keeps `.oc`; otherwise it does nothing. No verb breaks another's lock: 001 §E offers read-only instead. (3) `KitProxy.createInMemory`: the n-th proxy built over one `InMemoryKitFolder`, counting from 1, is owned by `{ machine = MachineName "in-memory"; user = UserName "in-memory"; processId = ProcessId n }`. The folder holds the lock, which `paths` lists as `.oc/lock` under `.oc`. Every in-memory lock is live. (4) `KitProxy.create`: the owner is `Environment.MachineName`, `Environment.UserName` and `ProcessId.getCurrentProcessId ()`, read when the proxy is built. In `KitFolderStore.fs`, `type KitLockHolder with member this.lockFileText : Result<KitFileText, StorageError>` is `nodeOf this` by `canonicalFileText` (step 124), and `type KitFileText with member this.parseLockHolder : Result<KitLockHolder, StorageError>` binds it through `CanonicalJson.options` (`MalformedJsonErr`). No schema covers it, because `.oc/` is cache. A lock is stale when its text does not bind, or when it names this machine (`StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase`) and a process id with no running process (`Process.GetProcessById` raises `ArgumentException`); every other lock is live. Taking it writes the holder's text to `.oc/lock-<a new GUID>.tmp` and moves that file to `.oc/lock` without overwriting (`File.Move(tmp, lock, false)`), deleting a stale lock first, so two openers racing never both hold it: the one whose move fails deletes its `.tmp`, reads the winner and answers `KitLockedErr` of it. Releasing reads `.oc/lock` and deletes it only when it binds to this proxy's holder. IO exceptions are `KitIoErr` as in step 126. Tests. `KitTests` gains `a lock holder renders who holds the lock, on which machine and since when`: a holder of `UserName "lab"` on `MachineName "BENCH-1"`, process 4242, acquired at `DateTime(2026, 11, 3, 12, 0, 0, DateTimeKind.Utc)`, renders `lab on BENCH-1 (process 4242) since 2026-11-03 12:00:00 UTC`; and `every kit error renders its condition and its payload` covers `KitLockedErr`. New `KitProxyContract` facts, each over proxies A, B and C from `openProxy` on one initialised folder and times from `steppingClock`: `a second proxy cannot take a held lock and is told who holds it, until it is released`: A's `acquireLock` answers a holder; B's answers `KitLockedErr` of exactly that holder; after A's `releaseLock`, B's answers a holder of B's owner at B's time. `a proxy that holds the lock keeps it when it asks again, and another proxy's release leaves it`: A's second `acquireLock` answers A's first holder, its time unchanged; B's `releaseLock` answers `Ok`, and C's `acquireLock` still answers `KitLockedErr` of A's holder. `the lock is written in the cache folder only when it is first taken, and its release keeps the folder`: after `initialiseKit`, `paths` holds no `.oc`; after A's `acquireLock` it holds `.oc` and `.oc/lock`; after A's `releaseLock` it holds `.oc` and no `.oc/lock`. `KitProxyFolderTests` gains, each writing `.oc/lock` itself through `lockFileText` where a holder is needed: `the folder lock names this machine, this user and this process`: the holder `acquireLock` answers is owned by `Environment.MachineName`, `Environment.UserName` and this process, and `.oc/lock` parses to it; `a lock left on this machine by a process that has ended is taken over`: a lock of this machine and process 2147483647 (`Int32.MaxValue`, never a Windows process id, which are multiples of 4) is replaced, and `acquireLock` answers this proxy's holder; `a lock taken on another machine is live`: a lock of `MachineName "another-machine"` and this process answers `KitLockedErr` of it and stays; and `an unreadable lock is taken over`: a `.oc/lock` holding `not a lock` is replaced, and `acquireLock` answers this proxy's holder.

**Acceptance.** After the lock lands, a second `KitProxy` over a locked kit MUST answer `KitLockedErr` carrying the holder while the first holds it, in both implementations, and the folder lock MUST be taken without overwriting a live one; constructor-unit-tests MUST pass, including `a lock holder renders who holds the lock, on which machine and since when`, the widened `every kit error renders its condition and its payload`, `a second proxy cannot take a held lock and is told who holds it, until it is released`, `a proxy that holds the lock keeps it when it asks again, and another proxy's release leaves it`, `the lock is written in the cache folder only when it is first taken, and its release keeps the folder` under `KitProxyTests` and `KitProxyFolderTests`, `the folder lock names this machine, this user and this process`, `a lock left on this machine by a process that has ended is taken over`, `a lock taken on another machine is live`, `an unreadable lock is taken over`, the count rising by 11; and build, elevation and the eleven s15 invariants MUST pass.
